      "text": "To solve the expression \\(1 - 4 + 7 - 10 + 13 - \\cdots - 46 + 49 - 52 + 55\\), we first observe the pattern in the sequence. The sequence alternates between positive and negative terms, and the terms are of the form \\( (4k-3) \\) for positive \\( k \\) and \\( -(4k-3) \\) for negative \\( k \\).\n\nThe sequence can be written as:\n\\[\n(1 - 4 + 7 - 10 + 13 - \\cdots - 46 + 49 - 52 + 55)\n\\]\n\nTo find the number of terms in the sequence, we note that the sequence starts at 1 and ends at 55, with each term increasing by 3. The general term of the sequence can be written as \\( 4k - 3 \\), where \\( k \\) is an integer. Setting the last term equal to 55, we get:\n\\[\n4k - 3 = 55 \\implies 4k = 58 \\implies k = 14.5\n\\]\nSince \\( k \\) must be an integer, the last term is actually \\( 4 \\times 14 - 3 = 52 \\). Therefore, the sequence has \\( 14 \\) terms (1 to 14, with each pair contributing two terms).\n\nWe can pair the terms as follows:\n\\[\n(1 - 4) + (7 - 10) + (13 - 16) + (19 - 22) + (25 - 28) + (31 - 34) + (37 - 40) + (43 - 46) + (49 - 52) + 55\n\\]\nEach pair \\( (4k-3) - (4k-1) = 2 \\), so there are \\( 14/2 = 7 \\) pairs of 2's. The last term, 55, is positive, making the entire sum positive.\n\nThus, the sum of the sequence is:\n\\[\n7 \\times 2 + 55 = 14 + 55 = 69\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the expression is \\(\\boxed{69}\\).",
